    "instruction_template": "Skill: Insert Structured Event\nPurpose: Log discrete events, state changes, and new entity introductions as structured memory items to prevent data fragmentation and enable reliable aggregation/counting over time. This directly addresses failures where opening turns are truncated and cumulative state tracking breaks down.\nWhen to use:\n- The text announces a new event, plan, trip, or life update.\n- The text introduces a new entity (e.g., pet, project, item) or changes the state/count of an existing one.\n- The text contains temporal markers linked to an action or state change.\n- The text includes the opening 1-3 turns of a session, which frequently contain primary announcements.\nHow to apply:\n1. PRESERVE KEY TURNS: Explicitly scan the first 3 utterances of the session. Do not truncate or skip them, even if brief. These often contain the primary event or temporal anchor.\n2. IDENTIFY & EXTRACT: Locate discrete events, entity introductions, or state updates. Extract exact names, dates, counts, and locations. Do not paraphrase critical values.\n3. STRUCTURE: Format the memory item using a strict, machine-parseable schema to support future aggregation. Use exactly this format:\n[EVENT_LOG] Type: <event_category> | Entity: <entity_name> | Date/Time: <normalized_temporal_marker> | State/Details: <specific_fact_or_change>\n4. SPLIT FOR AGGREGATION: Each log entry must represent a single, countable unit or distinct state change. If multiple entities/events are mentioned, split them into separate INSERT actions.\n5. VALIDATE: Compare against retrieved memories. Only insert if the event/state is new or adds missing temporal/factual precision. Do not duplicate existing logs.\nConstraints:\n- Output ONLY INSERT actions.\n- Do NOT merge multiple distinct events into one log entry.\n- Maintain the exact [EVENT_LOG] schema for all outputs to facilitate structured parsing and counting.\n- Only skip purely conversational filler with zero factual content.\nAction type: INSERT only.\n\nOutput Format:\nACTION: INSERT\nMEMORY_ITEM: [EVENT_LOG] Type: <type> | Entity: <entity> | Date/Time: <time> | State/Details: <details>\nREASONING: <why this action>",
