    "instruction_template": "Skill: Insert Temporal Anchor\nPurpose: Ingest events and facts with strict temporal normalization to ensure all stored memories have explicit, absolute dates. This prevents retrieval failures caused by relative time markers ('yesterday', 'last week') or implicit present-tense events lacking temporal anchors.\nWhen to use:\n- The text contains relative temporal markers (e.g., 'yesterday', 'last month', 'a year ago').\n- The text describes an event in the present tense or as just completed (e.g., 'I finally opened my shop') without an explicit date.\n- The text contains specific dates or times that need to be preserved and normalized.\nHow to apply:\n1. IDENTIFY TEMPORAL CONTEXT: Scan the text for explicit dates, relative time expressions, or implicit present-tense event declarations.\n2. NORMALIZE TO ABSOLUTE DATE: Convert all relative time expressions into absolute dates based on the current session's timestamp (e.g., if the session is on Nov 22, 'yesterday' becomes 'November 21').\n3. ANCHOR IMPLICIT EVENTS: If an event is described in the present tense or as newly completed without a specific time, automatically assign the session's absolute timestamp as the default temporal anchor.\n4. FORMULATE MEMORY: Construct a complete, standalone sentence that includes the normalized absolute date and the core fact. (e.g., 'On November 21, the user went to the store.' or 'On November 22, the user opened their shop.')\n5. DEDUPLICATE: Compare against retrieved memories to avoid duplicates.\nConstraints:\n- NEVER store raw relative time expressions like 'yesterday' or 'recently' without resolving them to an absolute date.\n- NEVER store an event without a temporal anchor; always append the session date if none is provided.\n- Output ONLY INSERT actions.\nAction type: INSERT only.\n\nOutput Format:\nACTION: INSERT\nMEMORY_ITEM: <content with absolute temporal anchor>\nREASONING: <temporal normalization logic and fact extraction>",
